{
  "$schema": "https://turbo.build/schema.json",
  "globalDependencies": ["**/.env.*local"],
  "tasks": {
    "build": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      // Turbo strips undeclared variables from a task's environment.
      // NEXT_PUBLIC_* reaches the Next builds through framework inference;
      // these three do not, and withSentryConfig reads them at build time to
      // upload source maps. Declaring them also puts them in the cache key, so
      // a build made without an auth token is not reused for one made with it.
      //
      // CONVEX_SITE_URL is here for a different reason than the other three:
      // nothing in a build reads it today, but `.github/workflows/ci.yml` pins
      // it on the Build step — deliberately, to keep a real backend URL out of
      // a compile-check bundle — and that line was inert, because an undeclared
      // variable never crosses this boundary. Declaring it makes the pin real
      // rather than decorative, and makes the day something does read it a
      // non-event.
      "env": ["SENTRY_ORG", "SENTRY_PROJECT", "SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN", "CONVEX_SITE_URL"],
      "passThroughEnv": ["CI"],
      "outputs": ["dist/**", ".next/**", "!.next/cache/**"]
    },
    "dev": {
      "cache": false,
      "persistent": true
    },
    // No `env` on lint or type-check on purpose, and this comment is the
    // answer to the next person who notices the asymmetry with `build` and
    // `test`. ESLint and tsc read no project variable: measured by scanning
    // every config and plugin in the repo for `process.env`, and the three
    // repository checks folded into the Lint job (`check:accents`,
    // `check:divergence`, `check:mirror-css`) are separate steps in ci.yml that
    // never travel through turbo. `CI` reaches every task regardless — turbo
    // treats it as a system variable, which was measured rather than assumed.
    "lint": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    "type-check": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"]
    },
    "test": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      // ── What the suite reads that is not in its own package ──────────────
      //
      // Turbo's default input set is the package's own git-tracked files. This
      // task's suites read far outside that, and until this list existed NONE
      // of it was hashed — so `Test` replayed a cached green over a tree that
      // had genuinely moved. Measured with `turbo run test --dry=json`, which
      // prints the hash without running anything: appending one newline to
      // `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, to the root `package.json`, to
      // `pnpm-lock.yaml`, to `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, to `CLAUDE.md`, to
      // `scripts/publish-mirror.mjs`, to `apps/themes/package.json` or to this
      // very file left `@beyours/reference#test`'s hash byte-for-byte identical.
      // So did bumping `packages/mcp-server/package.json` to 99.9.9, and so did
      // adding a changeset — which is #392 exactly: a version bump anywhere
      // replays a green `Test` over a drifted tree.
      //
      // The irony was sharpest one file away: `turbo-test-env.test.ts` reads
      // this `turbo.json` to keep the `env` list honest, and editing
      // `turbo.json` did not invalidate the test that reads `turbo.json`.
      //
      // `$TURBO_DEFAULT$` keeps the package's own files; everything after it is
      // added. `../../` is the repository root from any workspace, and that is
      // structural rather than lucky: `pnpm-workspace.yaml` declares exactly
      // `packages/*` and `apps/*`, so every workspace sits at depth two. A glob
      // that matches nothing is ignored, so a workspace added at another depth
      // would lose these silently — `turbo-test-inputs.test.ts` asserts the
      // depth rather than trusting it.
      //
      // The cost is deliberate. This list is wider than any single suite needs,
      // because it is one list shared by thirteen workspaces, and an input that
      // is too wide only re-runs tests that would have passed. One that is too
      // narrow reports a green for code nobody ran, which is the failure this
      // repository keeps paying for. `apps/themes/**` is the expensive entry
      // and it is not optional: `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts` and
      // `mirror-typecheck.test.ts` walk that tree.
      //
      // Not closed by this list, and worth stating rather than implying:
      // `engine-doc-imports.test.ts` reads every `.ts`/`.tsx` under
      // `packages/`. For an app those arrive through `dependsOn: ["^build"]`,
      // which puts each dependency's hash in this one — measured: editing
      // `packages/ui/src/index.ts` DOES move `@beyours/reference#test`. The
      // exception is `mcp-server`, which nothing depends on, so it is named
      // below.
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        // Read by `turbo-test-env.test.ts` and by this task's own definition.
        "../../turbo.json",
        // Root manifest, lockfile and workspace globs: `mirror-overrides.test.ts`
        // parses the lockfile's `overrides` block as TEXT, and
        // `workflow-publish-gates.test.ts` and `client-stylesheet.test.ts` read
        // the root manifest. Turbo hashes RESOLVED dependencies from the
        // lockfile, not the file, so a suite reading the bytes was never covered.
        "../../package.json",
        "../../pnpm-lock.yaml",
        "../../pnpm-workspace.yaml",
        // `engine-doc-imports.test.ts` compiles the import lines in these.
        "../../CLAUDE.md",
        "../../README.md",
        // `pending-release.test.ts`, through `scripts/lib/pending-release.mjs`,
        // which resolves `CHANGESET_DIR` from its own URL — a read no scan of
        // the test source can see.
        "../../.changeset/**",
        // `workflow-publish-gates.test.ts` reads every workflow and composite
        // action; `client-stylesheet.test.ts` asserts on `ci.yml`;
        // `engine-version-diagnostic.test.ts` reads the template's own CI.
        "../../.github/**",
        // `mirror-publisher.test.ts`, `mirror-typecheck.test.ts`,
        // `mirror-overrides.test.ts`, `publish-plan.test.ts`,
        // `source-drift.test.ts` and `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts` all read
        // or import these.
        "../../scripts/**",
        // The version bump itself — the headline of #392. Read by
        // `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts`, `publish-plan.test.ts`,
        // `client-stylesheet.test.ts`, `mirror-overrides.test.ts`,
        // `mirror-typecheck.test.ts` and `registry.test.ts`.
        "../../packages/*/package.json",
        "../../packages/*/tsup.config.ts",
        "../../apps/*/package.json",
        "../../apps/*/tsconfig.json",
        // Named because nothing depends on it, so `^build` cannot carry it:
        // `mcp-registry-imports.test.ts` reads the registry source and
        // `engine-doc-imports.test.ts` walks every `.md` and every `.ts`/`.tsx`
        // under `packages/`. Sibling packages arrive through
        // `dependsOn: ["^build"]`; `mcp-server` has no dependents, so it is
        // spelled out.
        //
        // Three globs rather than `mcp-server/**`, and the difference is not
        // style: a whole-package glob dragged `node_modules/.bin/*` and
        // `node_modules/.vite/vitest/*/results.json` into the key, and the
        // negations below could not take them back out. Naming what the suites
        // read cannot pick up an artefact by accident.
        "../../packages/mcp-server/src/**",
        "../../packages/mcp-server/*.md",
        "../../packages/mcp-server/*.ts",
        // The tree the mirror publishes. `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts` walks
        // it whole and `mirror-typecheck.test.ts` compiles it.
        "../../apps/themes/**",
        // ── and NOT the artefacts inside those two ────────────────────────────
        //
        // An explicit `inputs` glob does not consult `.gitignore`. Turbo's
        // DEFAULT set does, which is why nobody meets this until the first
        // explicit glob is written — and then it is a silent disaster rather
        // than a loud one.
        //
        // `apps/themes/**` pulled in `node_modules/.vite/vitest/*/results.json`,
        // which is Vitest's cache of the PREVIOUS run's per-test durations. So
        // `pnpm test` rewrote one of its own inputs on every run: three floats
        // change, thirteen `#test` hashes change, and the cache could never hit
        // twice. Measured — one duration edited from 545.435 to 999.999 moved
        // `@be-in-digital/core#test` from `a557b1b5dc87ac53` to
        // `cfaa28aabb923c1e`. The `node_modules/.bin/*` symlinks came too, which
        // put resolved transitive versions in the key as a bonus.
        //
        // An input that is not a function of the source tree is worse than a
        // missing one: it does not report a false green, it destroys the cache.
        //
        // `**/` rather than `../../apps/themes/...`, and that is measured, not
        // taste: turbo normalises an input path against the package it is
        // resolving for, so `../../apps/themes` becomes `../themes` from
        // `apps/reference` and stays `../../apps/themes` from `packages/core` —
        // and a negation written in one of those forms silently failed to match
        // the other. `@be-in-digital/core#test` came out clean while
        // `@beyours/reference#test` still carried the vitest cache. The rooted
        // `**/` form has no depth to disagree about.
        "!**/node_modules/**",
        "!**/.next/**",
        "!**/.turbo/**",
        "!**/coverage/**",
        "!**/test-results/**",
        "!**/playwright-report/**"
      ],
      // `env`, not `passThroughEnv`, and the difference is the whole point.
      //
      // With `globalEnvMode` at its default of `strict`, an undeclared variable
      // is stripped before vitest starts — so the three Deliveroo suites that
      // self-skip on `it.runIf(hasWebhookTarget)` / `hasDeliverooSandbox`
      // (`scenario-02`, `scenario-08`, `menu/scenario-01`, in each of the two
      // apps) skipped no matter what was set in the repository secrets. Setting
      // a GitHub secret could not make a gated suite run, which is a worse
      // failure than the suite being off: the secret exists, it looks
      // load-bearing, and the run is green either way.
      //
      // `tasks/ci-required-checks-runbook.md` §7 calls it "the 11 Deliveroo
      // suites". Counted: twelve files under `apps/*/e2e/deliveroo`, three of
      // which gate on credentials. The other nine assert on signatures and
      // mappings offline and always ran.
      //
      // `passThroughEnv` would have let the value through and left the second
      // half open. This task is CACHED — a turbo task with no `outputs` still
      // replays its success without running the command — and a passed-through
      // variable is not part of the cache key, so the first run after a secret
      // appeared would have replayed the result of the run that skipped
      // everything. In `env` the value is in the key: turning a secret on
      // changes the hash, the suite genuinely re-runs, and the skip becomes a
      // pass or a failure.
      //
      // `apps/reference/__tests__/turbo-test-env.test.ts` holds this list to
      // what the suites actually read. Adding `process.env.SOMETHING` to a test
      // without adding it here fails that guard rather than silently reading
      // undefined.
      "env": [
        // The Deliveroo scenario suites under `apps/*/e2e/deliveroo` — Vitest,
        // not Playwright, and the reason this task needed fixing at all.
        "CONVEX_SITE_URL",
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_SITE_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_BRAND_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_E2E_REQUIRE_LIVE",
        "DELIVEROO_IS_SANDBOX",
        "SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL",
        "SEED_PASSWORD",
        // Uber Eats and the payment providers: sandbox flags and the webhook
        // secrets the signature suites verify against.
        "UBER_EATS_CLIENT_ID",
        "UBER_EATS_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "UBER_EATS_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "UBER_EATS_SANDBOX_MODE",
        "PAYPAL_SANDBOX_MODE",
        // Read by the cardPaymentAvailability suite: the def mirrors the
        // charge action's own key check, so the test sets and reads it.
        "STRIPE_SECRET_KEY",
        "STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        // AWS, read by the S3 and SES suites.
        "AWS_REGION",
        "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID",
        "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
        "AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME",
        "AWS_SES_CONFIGURATION_SET",
        // The rest.
        "ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN",
        "OPENAI_API_KEY",
        // The commercial site's deliberate no-payment path. Declared so the
        // suites that need it OFF can actually turn it off: `checkoutSavedCard`
        // exercises the live Stripe branch, which returns early when this is
        // "true", and an undeclared variable cannot be deleted from an
        // environment it never reached. Its value is not a fixture — a
        // developer with it set locally must get the same result as CI.
        "BEYOURS_TEST_CHECKOUT",
        // The Convex-side Sentry DSN. The reporting suites set it to a fixture
        // DSN and read it back through `resolveSentryOptions`, so it has to
        // survive the boundary — an undeclared variable reads `undefined`, the
        // resolver returns `null`, and every assertion about a report leaving
        // the process would pass while nothing left.
        "SENTRY_DSN",
        // NEXT_PUBLIC_* already crosses the boundary — turbo passes the prefix
        // through unconditionally, measured. They are named anyway so they
        // belong to the CACHE KEY: a suite run against one VAT stance must not
        // be replayed for another.
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL",
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL",
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_TVA_ENABLED"
      ],
      "passThroughEnv": ["CI"]
    },
    // `pnpm test:coverage` is the only task that writes `coverage/`. It used
    // to be declared as an output of `test`, which produces nothing — a cache
    // slot for an artefact no command created, next to a CLAUDE.md line
    // promising "80%+ coverage" that nothing measured.
    //
    // No threshold is enforced. The number is reported, not gated; see
    // TESTING.md before adding one, because a threshold set above what the
    // suite currently reaches turns every unrelated pull request red.
    //
    // The env list below MUST match `test`'s, and is duplicated because
    // turbo.json has no way to share one — it runs the same suites, so a
    // variable stripped here skips the same three Deliveroo suites, just with
    // a coverage report attached. `turbo-test-env.test.ts` asserts the two
    // lists are equal, so drift fails rather than being noticed a year later.
    "test:coverage": {
      "dependsOn": ["^build"],
      // The same list as `test`'s, and duplicated for the same reason its `env`
      // list is: turbo.json has no way to share one. It runs the same suites,
      // so an input missing here replays the same false green with a coverage
      // report attached. `turbo-test-inputs.test.ts` asserts the two are equal.
      "inputs": [
        "$TURBO_DEFAULT$",
        // Read by `turbo-test-env.test.ts` and by this task's own definition.
        "../../turbo.json",
        // Root manifest, lockfile and workspace globs: `mirror-overrides.test.ts`
        // parses the lockfile's `overrides` block as TEXT, and
        // `workflow-publish-gates.test.ts` and `client-stylesheet.test.ts` read
        // the root manifest. Turbo hashes RESOLVED dependencies from the
        // lockfile, not the file, so a suite reading the bytes was never covered.
        "../../package.json",
        "../../pnpm-lock.yaml",
        "../../pnpm-workspace.yaml",
        // `engine-doc-imports.test.ts` compiles the import lines in these.
        "../../CLAUDE.md",
        "../../README.md",
        // `pending-release.test.ts`, through `scripts/lib/pending-release.mjs`,
        // which resolves `CHANGESET_DIR` from its own URL — a read no scan of
        // the test source can see.
        "../../.changeset/**",
        // `workflow-publish-gates.test.ts` reads every workflow and composite
        // action; `client-stylesheet.test.ts` asserts on `ci.yml`;
        // `engine-version-diagnostic.test.ts` reads the template's own CI.
        "../../.github/**",
        // `mirror-publisher.test.ts`, `mirror-typecheck.test.ts`,
        // `mirror-overrides.test.ts`, `publish-plan.test.ts`,
        // `source-drift.test.ts` and `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts` all read
        // or import these.
        "../../scripts/**",
        // The version bump itself — the headline of #392. Read by
        // `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts`, `publish-plan.test.ts`,
        // `client-stylesheet.test.ts`, `mirror-overrides.test.ts`,
        // `mirror-typecheck.test.ts` and `registry.test.ts`.
        "../../packages/*/package.json",
        "../../packages/*/tsup.config.ts",
        "../../apps/*/package.json",
        "../../apps/*/tsconfig.json",
        // Named because nothing depends on it, so `^build` cannot carry it:
        // `mcp-registry-imports.test.ts` reads the registry source and
        // `engine-doc-imports.test.ts` walks every `.md` and every `.ts`/`.tsx`
        // under `packages/`. Sibling packages arrive through
        // `dependsOn: ["^build"]`; `mcp-server` has no dependents, so it is
        // spelled out.
        //
        // Three globs rather than `mcp-server/**`, and the difference is not
        // style: a whole-package glob dragged `node_modules/.bin/*` and
        // `node_modules/.vite/vitest/*/results.json` into the key, and the
        // negations below could not take them back out. Naming what the suites
        // read cannot pick up an artefact by accident.
        "../../packages/mcp-server/src/**",
        "../../packages/mcp-server/*.md",
        "../../packages/mcp-server/*.ts",
        // The tree the mirror publishes. `mirror-engine-exports.test.ts` walks
        // it whole and `mirror-typecheck.test.ts` compiles it.
        "../../apps/themes/**",
        // ── and NOT the artefacts inside those two ────────────────────────────
        //
        // An explicit `inputs` glob does not consult `.gitignore`. Turbo's
        // DEFAULT set does, which is why nobody meets this until the first
        // explicit glob is written — and then it is a silent disaster rather
        // than a loud one.
        //
        // `apps/themes/**` pulled in `node_modules/.vite/vitest/*/results.json`,
        // which is Vitest's cache of the PREVIOUS run's per-test durations. So
        // `pnpm test` rewrote one of its own inputs on every run: three floats
        // change, thirteen `#test` hashes change, and the cache could never hit
        // twice. Measured — one duration edited from 545.435 to 999.999 moved
        // `@be-in-digital/core#test` from `a557b1b5dc87ac53` to
        // `cfaa28aabb923c1e`. The `node_modules/.bin/*` symlinks came too, which
        // put resolved transitive versions in the key as a bonus.
        //
        // An input that is not a function of the source tree is worse than a
        // missing one: it does not report a false green, it destroys the cache.
        //
        // `**/` rather than `../../apps/themes/...`, and that is measured, not
        // taste: turbo normalises an input path against the package it is
        // resolving for, so `../../apps/themes` becomes `../themes` from
        // `apps/reference` and stays `../../apps/themes` from `packages/core` —
        // and a negation written in one of those forms silently failed to match
        // the other. `@be-in-digital/core#test` came out clean while
        // `@beyours/reference#test` still carried the vitest cache. The rooted
        // `**/` form has no depth to disagree about.
        "!**/node_modules/**",
        "!**/.next/**",
        "!**/.turbo/**",
        "!**/coverage/**",
        "!**/test-results/**",
        "!**/playwright-report/**"
      ],
      "env": [
        // The Deliveroo scenario suites under `apps/*/e2e/deliveroo` — Vitest,
        // not Playwright, and the reason this task needed fixing at all.
        "CONVEX_SITE_URL",
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_SITE_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_BRAND_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_E2E_REQUIRE_LIVE",
        "DELIVEROO_IS_SANDBOX",
        "SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL",
        "SEED_PASSWORD",
        // Uber Eats and the payment providers: sandbox flags and the webhook
        // secrets the signature suites verify against.
        "UBER_EATS_CLIENT_ID",
        "UBER_EATS_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "UBER_EATS_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "UBER_EATS_SANDBOX_MODE",
        "PAYPAL_SANDBOX_MODE",
        // Read by the cardPaymentAvailability suite: the def mirrors the
        // charge action's own key check, so the test sets and reads it.
        "STRIPE_SECRET_KEY",
        "STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        // AWS, read by the S3 and SES suites.
        "AWS_REGION",
        "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID",
        "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
        "AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME",
        "AWS_SES_CONFIGURATION_SET",
        // The rest.
        "ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN",
        "OPENAI_API_KEY",
        // The commercial site's deliberate no-payment path. Declared so the
        // suites that need it OFF can actually turn it off: `checkoutSavedCard`
        // exercises the live Stripe branch, which returns early when this is
        // "true", and an undeclared variable cannot be deleted from an
        // environment it never reached. Its value is not a fixture — a
        // developer with it set locally must get the same result as CI.
        "BEYOURS_TEST_CHECKOUT",
        // The Convex-side Sentry DSN. The reporting suites set it to a fixture
        // DSN and read it back through `resolveSentryOptions`, so it has to
        // survive the boundary — an undeclared variable reads `undefined`, the
        // resolver returns `null`, and every assertion about a report leaving
        // the process would pass while nothing left.
        "SENTRY_DSN",
        // NEXT_PUBLIC_* already crosses the boundary — turbo passes the prefix
        // through unconditionally, measured. They are named anyway so they
        // belong to the CACHE KEY: a suite run against one VAT stance must not
        // be replayed for another.
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL",
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL",
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_TVA_ENABLED"
      ],
      "passThroughEnv": ["CI"],
      "outputs": ["coverage/**"]
    },
    "test:e2e": {
      "dependsOn": ["build"],
      "cache": false,
      // The e2e job is the one task whose command spawns a *server*, and strict
      // mode strips everything below before Playwright ever starts it.
      //
      // `.github/workflows/e2e.yml` sets all of these in the job environment,
      // and CI calls `pnpm test:e2e` from the root — so they travel through
      // turbo, which dropped them. `next start` then failed its own env check
      // ("10 environment variable(s) missing or invalid"), the web server never
      // came up, and the run died on the 120 s webServer timeout with zero
      // tests executed. Every local run went through `cd apps/reference`
      // instead, which is why nothing ever caught it.
      //
      // passThroughEnv, not env: this task is uncached, so there is no key for
      // these to belong to.
      "passThroughEnv": [
        "CI",
        "E2E_USE_BUILD",
        // The suite: which account to sign in as, and with what password.
        "SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL",
        "SEED_PASSWORD",
        // The required tier of `packages/core/src/env/schemas.ts`, which
        // instrumentation.ts enforces at startup and refuses to serve without.
        "NEXT_PUBLIC_CONVEX_URL",
        "CONVEX_SITE_URL",
        "CONVEX_DEPLOYMENT",
        "SITE_URL",
        "BETTER_AUTH_SECRET",
        "BETTER_AUTH_URL",
        "ENCRYPTION_KEY",
        "AWS_S3_BUCKET_NAME",
        "AWS_SES_FROM_EMAIL",
        "AWS_REGION",
        "AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID",
        "AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY",
        "OPENAI_API_KEY",
        // Read by the Deliveroo suites, which skip themselves when unset.
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_CLIENT_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_WEBHOOK_SECRET",
        "DELIVEROO_SITE_ID",
        "DELIVEROO_BRAND_ID"
      ]
    },
    "clean": {
      "cache": false
    }
  }
}
